  "text": "As a lighting and set designer, explain how you would design the visual environment for a scene in which a figure of institutional authority interrogates a vulnerable character. Your design should reflect Brechtian staging principles. Refer to specific design choices and explain their effect on the audience.",
  "type": "8 marks — Interpret as Designer",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8): Specific, named design choices are made for both set and lighting that clearly reflect Brechtian principles. Set might include exposed scaffolding, visible backstage space, a raised platform for the authority figure to create levels, and visible rigging — communicating that this is constructed theatre, not a real room. Lighting might use unfiltered profile lanterns rigged in view, a hard-edged beam on the authority figure from directly above (high angle, creating harsh shadow), and a flat cold wash on the vulnerable character, with no soft naturalistic fill. The designer explains how these choices serve the V-effect: the audience is kept at a critical distance, reading the scene as a statement about institutional power rather than being absorbed in the individual emotional exchange. Effect on the audience is named.\n\nSecure (5-6): Both set and lighting are addressed with reasonably specific choices. Brechtian principles are referenced. Effect on audience is mentioned.\n\nDeveloping (3-4): Design choices are present but generic ('harsh lighting, bare set'). Limited connection to Brechtian intention.\n\nEmerging (1-2): Very general design ideas with little or no reference to Brechtian techniques or effect on audience."
 },
 {
  "text": "Analyse how Brecht's use of songs interrupting the action reveals his intentions for the audience. What is Brecht trying to make the audience think, feel or understand through this technique?",
  "type": "12 marks — Analyse Intentions",
  "marks": "Mastering (10-12): The response analyses how songs function as a V-effect device, breaking emotional absorption at the peak of a scene and returning the audience to a critical position. Brecht's didactic intention is clearly articulated: theatre is a tool for social change; the audience must be kept alert, judgement intact, in order to act differently in the world. The response may analyse how a song's lyrical content offers an ironic commentary on the dramatic situation, or how the shift in theatrical register (from dramatic scene to performed number) makes visible the constructed nature of what they are watching. Multiple functions of the technique are explored, not just one. The analysis connects the technique to Brecht's political philosophy.\n\nSecure (7-9): Brecht's intentions are explained with reference to the V-effect and the idea of keeping the audience thinking. At least one function of songs is analysed with explanation of audience effect.\n\nDeveloping (4-6): Songs are described as a technique but the analysis focuses on what they do to the story rather than what they do to the audience's critical relationship to the play.\n\nEmerging (1-3): Songs are mentioned as a feature of Brecht's work but no analytical development of intention or audience effect."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are directing a production that applies Brechtian staging principles throughout. Explain how you would stage a scene in which a character must make a moral choice under pressure from those in authority. In your answer, refer to: the staging configuration, the use of gestus by performers, at least two specific design disciplines (lighting, sound, set or costume), and the effect you intend on the audience.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering (17-20): The response applies Brechtian principles consistently and specifically across multiple staging disciplines. The performer playing the authority figure demonstrates a clearly described gestus (e.g. slow, deliberate speech; a physical stillness that occupies space without moving; a costume that signals institutional role — uniform, wig, gown — over a plain underscore). The character under pressure has a contrasting gestus (e.g. contained, inward, reduced physical footprint). Staging configuration is chosen and justified (e.g. traverse staging so the audience sit on both sides, making them feel complicit in witnessing the interrogation). Set design is spare and Brechtian: visible structure, a raised platform for authority, a plain chair centre stage as the only furniture, meaning concentrated by absence. Lighting uses profile lanterns rigged visibly at end-on angles, a hard front-light on the authority figure and a cooler, lower-intensity beam on the character under pressure, with no naturalistic fill. Sound uses live percussion visible in the corner, building a rhythm under the interrogation that gets slower as pressure mounts, then stops entirely at the decision moment — the silence is as deliberate as any sound. A placard or projection announces the outcome of the scene before it begins. The response explains what the audience is intended to think and feel: the V-effect keeps them critical, the gestus signals systemic power rather than individual villainy.\n\nSecure (13-16): Most Brechtian elements are present and staged with reasonable specificity. At least three design disciplines are addressed. Gestus is used for at least one performer. Audience effect is explained.\n\nDeveloping (8-12): Relevant Brechtian ideas are applied but may be thin on specificity or miss one or more design disciplines. Gestus may be named but not fully demonstrated in practice.\n\nEmerging (1-7): Some knowledge of Brecht is shown. Design choices are vague. Limited application of specific techniques."
