=== L4 Physical Theatre: Frantic Assembly and Beyond ===
id: e1181eda-2434-4d9d-a159-16ba085cf9dc
slug: physical-theatre-frantic-assembly-and-beyond
status: live tier:both updated:2026-07-30T22:54:21.405837+00:00
narration_manifest: array len 24

--- DESCRIPTION ---
Movement-led storytelling, ensemble, lifts, chair duets and how physical theatre communicates without words.

--- CONTENT_HTML ---
<h2 data-narration-id="n1">What Is Physical Theatre?</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n2"><dfn class="term" data-def="A style of performance in which movement and the body are the primary storytelling tools, with meaning communicated through choreographed physical sequences rather than predominantly through spoken dialogue.">Physical theatre</dfn> is a broad term for performance in which the body is the dominant instrument of communication. It does not mean theatre without words &mdash; most physical theatre companies use spoken text &mdash; but the physical vocabulary of the performers carries equal or greater meaning than the language they speak. Physical theatre draws on dance, acrobatics, mime, martial arts, Lecoq technique, contact improvisation and a range of other movement traditions to create a distinctive grammar of the body.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n3">Physical theatre companies have shaped contemporary British and international theatre profoundly. The four most relevant for GCSE Drama are Frantic Assembly, Complicite, DV8 and Steven Berkoff &mdash; each with a distinctive approach to how physical language communicates character, relationship, status and theme.</p>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n4" data-revision-tip="Without looking, name four physical theatre companies or practitioners and write one distinctive feature of each approach.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Frantic Assembly are known for their &ldquo;round-by-through-under&rdquo; methodology of exploring physical contact and for chair duets as a signature technique. Complicite are known for object transformation and ensemble storytelling. DV8 make physical theatre that addresses social and political issues through extreme physicality. Berkoff uses highly stylised, exaggerated movement as a form of social satire.</p>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n5">Frantic Assembly: Round-by-Through-Under and Ensemble</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n6">Frantic Assembly was founded in 1994 by Scott Graham and Steven Hoggett. Their signature is a devising methodology that begins with physical exploration rather than textual analysis &mdash; performers explore the physical relationship between bodies before the meaning of the scene is fixed. The central physical framework they use is <dfn class="term" data-def="Frantic Assembly's signature movement devising method: performers explore every possible physical path around, through and under each other's bodies to build a movement vocabulary for a scene.">round-by-through-under</dfn>: performers explore every possible way their bodies can move in relation to another performer &mdash; over, under, around, through &mdash; generating a range of physical possibilities before selecting the most expressive for the final sequence.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n7">Their most recognisable technique is the <dfn class="term" data-def="A physical theatre duet using chairs as props, in which two performers interact with and around their chairs to explore relationship, tension and emotion through movement rather than words.">chair duet</dfn>. Two performers use chairs as props and obstacles, their physical relationship to each other and to the furniture communicating the emotional state of the scene &mdash; desire, conflict, distance, dependence &mdash; without a single word. The chair duet has become a GCSE Drama shorthand for Frantic Assembly because it is both technically distinctive and a perfect demonstration of physical theatre&rsquo;s ability to communicate without language.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n8">Frantic Assembly co-developed <em>Things I Know to Be True</em> with playwright Andrew Bovell in 2016 &mdash; one of the nine AQA set plays. In that production, physical sequences were embedded within a naturalistic family drama, with moments of heightened physical theatre marking emotional revelations that ordinary speech could not carry.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Lifts and contact work: how physical support communicates relationship</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n9">Frantic Assembly use <dfn class="term" data-def="A physical technique in which one or more performers physically support and elevate another, used to communicate trust, power, vulnerability or relationship in physical theatre.">lifts</dfn> and physical support work to communicate relationships that are otherwise invisible. When one performer lifts another, the audience reads: this person holds this person up; this person trusts this person completely; this person&rsquo;s weight is carried by another. A lift in a scene of reconciliation communicates something different from a lift in a scene of control or coercion &mdash; but both are immediate, legible and viscerally felt by the audience in a way that dialogue rarely achieves. The physical vocabulary of weight, support, resistance and release is a direct language of human relationship.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<h2 data-narration-id="n10">Ensemble: Multi-Roling and Collective Voice</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n11">Physical theatre is fundamentally an <dfn class="term" data-def="A company of performers who work as a collective unit, often sharing roles and physical tasks equally rather than operating within a conventional star-and-supporting-cast hierarchy.">ensemble</dfn> art form. The ensemble does not simply mean &ldquo;the cast.&rdquo; In physical theatre, the ensemble is a finely tuned collective body &mdash; performers who have developed a shared physical language, a precise awareness of each other&rsquo;s timing, weight and spatial positioning. The ensemble can represent a crowd, a community, a family or a social force through synchronised or deliberately fragmented movement.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n12"><dfn class="term" data-def="When a performer plays more than one role in a production, often using costume, movement or vocal changes to signal the shift between characters.">Multi-roling</dfn> is common in physical theatre because the emphasis on the performer&rsquo;s body as a malleable instrument makes transformation between roles legible and immediate. A performer might shift from playing a child to an authority figure to a memory from the past in a single sequence, signalled not by a costume change but by a postural shift, a change in vocal pitch and pace, and a change in <dfn class="term" data-def="The use of different heights and vertical positioning on stage — raised platforms, floor level, mid-level — to communicate status, power and relationship.">levels</dfn> in the space.</p>

<h2 data-narration-id="n13">Complicite, DV8 and Berkoff</h2>
<p data-narration-id="n14">Complicite (founded by Simon McBurney in 1983) are known for <dfn class="term" data-def="A technique in physical theatre in which everyday objects are used to represent different things in different scenes, the meaning shifted by the performer's relationship to the object.">object transformation</dfn> and an ensemble storytelling style that blends visual theatre, physical comedy and emotional intensity. A single crate might become a bed, a car, a coffin, and a judge&rsquo;s bench within a single production, with the audience accepting each transformation because the performers&rsquo; physical commitment to the fiction is absolute.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n15">DV8 Physical Theatre (founded by Lloyd Newson in 1986) make work that uses extreme physicality to address social and political questions &mdash; masculinity, sexuality, violence, and the limits of the body. Their work is often uncomfortable, physically demanding and deliberately confrontational.</p>
<p data-narration-id="n16">Steven Berkoff developed a highly stylised physical theatre language influenced by Brecht, mime and Artaud: exaggerated gesture, stylised movement, heightened vocal delivery, and a theatrical artifice that is never disguised. Like Brecht, Berkoff uses the performer&rsquo;s <dfn class="term" data-def="The physical bearing, posture and way of holding the body that communicates a character's status, mood or social type.">posture</dfn> and movement to reveal social type and class position.</p>

<div class="collapsible">
  <button class="collapsible-toggle" aria-expanded="false">
    <span>Applying physical theatre: performer and designer examples for a set-play moment</span>
    <svg class="collapsible-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"/></svg>
  </button>
  <div class="collapsible-content"><div class="collapsible-inner">
    <p data-narration-id="n17"><strong>As a performer:</strong> Imagine you are staging the moment of a family argument in which unspoken resentments finally surface. Rather than having the performers simply shout at each other, you build a Frantic Assembly-influenced sequence: the two central performers begin with a round-by-through-under exploration of physical space, each movement growing more restricted and angular until one performer &mdash; positioned <dfn class="term" data-def="The area of the stage closest to the audience.">downstage</dfn> centre &mdash; executes a chair duet sequence with the other circling them. The ensemble of family members behind them mirror the central relationship in a reduced, stylised echo &mdash; the same physical tension played out collectively across the group. The scene ends with a tableau: all performers frozen in a configuration that places the two central figures back-to-back, hands touching but faces turned away. The audience reads the emotional state through bodies, not words.</p>
    <p data-narration-id="n18"><strong>As a designer:</strong> For a physical theatre sequence depicting emotional fracture within a group, you might design a <dfn class="term" data-def="A lighting effect created by passing light through a patterned template, casting a pattern of shadow and light onto the stage." >gobo</dfn> pattern that fragments the light on the floor &mdash; broken lines suggesting division &mdash; while a soundscape of overlapping voices (each speaking a different fragment of a shared text) builds under the movement. As the tableau is reached, the lighting narrows to a single tight <dfn class="term" data-def="A specific, focused lighting state directed at one area or performer, used to isolate or highlight.">lighting special</dfn> on the two central performers, the surrounding space falling into shadow, making the family&rsquo;s isolation physical and visible.</p>
  </div></div>
</div>

<div class="key-fact" data-narration-id="n19" data-revision-tip="Without looking, explain what a 'chair duet' is, what it communicates, and why Frantic Assembly use physical exploration before fixing meaning in a scene.">
  <div class="key-fact-label">Key Fact</div>
  <p>Physical theatre communicates emotion and relationship through the body &mdash; weight, support, resistance, proximity and movement vocabulary replace or supplement dialogue. In the exam, describe specific physical sequences, named techniques (chair duets, lifts, round-by-through-under, tableau, multi-roling) and explain precisely what each communicates to the audience about the characters or theme.</p>
</div>

--- CONCLUSION_HTML ---
<h3 data-narration-id="n21">Key Takeaways</h3>
<ul>
  <li data-narration-id="n22">Physical theatre uses the body as the primary storytelling instrument. Key Frantic Assembly techniques &mdash; round-by-through-under, chair duets, lifts, and ensemble tableau &mdash; communicate emotion and relationship through physical vocabulary, not dialogue.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n23">Multi-roling and ensemble work allow a small company to represent communities, families and social forces. Object transformation (Complicite) and extreme physicality (DV8) extend the range of what the physical theatre company can communicate.</li>
  <li data-narration-id="n24">In the exam, always name specific techniques, describe precise physical qualities (weight, height, speed, proximity), anchor choices to a named moment of the play, and explain what the audience understands from the physical language rather than from the words.</li>
</ul>

--- EXAM_TIP_HTML ---
<p data-narration-id="n20">On performer questions about physical theatre, the most common error is writing in vague terms: &ldquo;I would move slowly and dramatically to show the emotion.&rdquo; This scores nothing in the top band. You must specify the movement vocabulary: &ldquo;I would begin in a contracted posture, weight sinking low into my hips, then execute a slow lift in which my fellow performer rises above me at centre stage, arms extended, while I maintain a held stillness below &mdash; the lift communicating the character&rsquo;s need to elevate and protect someone they love, even as my own body registers the cost of that act.&rdquo; Name the technique (lift, chair duet, tableau, round-by-through-under), describe the specific physical qualities (height, weight, speed, resistance), name a moment in the play, and explain what the audience understands from the movement. For 20-mark questions, make sure you address both physical performer choices and at least two design disciplines, explaining how they work together to create a unified physical theatrical statement.</p>

--- PRACTICE_QUESTIONS ---
[
 {
  "text": "Name one physical theatre company that co-developed a GCSE Drama set play with the playwright.",
  "type": "1 mark — Identify",
  "marks": "1 mark for: Frantic Assembly (who co-developed Things I Know to Be True with Andrew Bovell)."
 },
 {
  "text": "What is meant by 'multi-roling' in physical theatre performance?",
  "type": "2 marks — Define",
  "marks": "1 mark for identifying that multi-roling means a performer plays more than one role in a production; 1 mark for explaining that the shift between roles is signalled through physical, vocal or costume changes rather than requiring separate actors."
 },
 {
  "text": "Explain how a Frantic Assembly chair duet communicates the emotional relationship between two characters to the audience.",
  "type": "4 marks — Explain Effect",
  "marks": "Up to 4 marks: 1 mark for identifying a chair duet as a physical theatre technique using chairs as props in a movement sequence; 1 mark for naming a specific physical quality of the technique (e.g. the proximity and movement of the two performers around the chairs, their weight and resistance); 1 mark for explaining how a specific physical quality communicates an emotional state (e.g. one performer circling the other while the other remains still communicates power imbalance or emotional withdrawal); 1 mark for explaining the effect on the audience (e.g. the audience reads the relationship through bodies rather than words, making the emotional state feel immediate and physical)."
 },
 {
  "text": "As a performer in a physical theatre production, explain how you would use physical theatre techniques — including at least one Frantic Assembly method — to stage the moment when a character realises they have lost someone they depended on. Refer to specific physical choices and explain their effect on the audience.",
  "type": "8 marks — Interpret as Performer",
  "marks": "Mastering (7-8): The response identifies a named physical theatre technique (e.g. a lift in which the other performer's weight is suddenly absent; a chair duet that ends with the performer alone; a tableau in which the ensemble turns their backs while the central performer faces the audience). Both physical qualities (specific weight, height, speed, spatial positioning) and the effect on the audience are described precisely. The response demonstrates understanding that physical theatre communicates through the body rather than through verbal announcement of emotion. At least one Frantic Assembly method is named and applied.\n\nSecure (5-6): A named technique is used and a named moment is referenced. Physical choices are described with some specificity. Effect on audience is explained.\n\nDeveloping (3-4): Physical choices are mentioned but described vaguely ('I would move slowly and show sadness'). Limited specific technique reference.\n\nEmerging (1-2): Basic description of movement with no named technique and no explanation of audience effect."
 },
 {
  "text": "Analyse how physical theatre companies like Frantic Assembly use the ensemble to communicate themes and social relationships that individual characters alone cannot express. What do directors of physical theatre intend the audience to understand through ensemble movement?",
  "type": "12 marks — Analyse Intentions",
  "marks": "Mastering (10-12): The response analyses how the ensemble functions as a collective body that can represent community, social force or shared experience. The director's intention is clearly articulated: by synchronising or fragmenting the ensemble's movement, the audience reads social relationships (unity, division, coercion, solidarity) in a way individual performances cannot achieve. The response may analyse how the ensemble mirrors central relationships (a chair duet framed by ensemble members repeating the same physical tension in miniature), how multi-roling within the ensemble allows social types to be embodied and challenged, and how the physical collective makes visible the social structures that surround individual characters. The connection to theatrical intention is sustained throughout.\n\nSecure (7-9): The ensemble's functions are explained with some analytical development. At least two functions of ensemble work are explored. Effect on audience is analysed.\n\nDeveloping (4-6): The ensemble is described correctly but analysis focuses on what it looks like rather than what it communicates or why the director uses it.\n\nEmerging (1-3): Basic description of ensemble as a group of performers with limited analysis of its communicative function."
 },
 {
  "text": "You are directing a physical theatre production of a play in which a family gradually fractures under the weight of unspoken truths. Explain how you would use physical theatre techniques — including ensemble movement, lifts or chair duets, and tableau — alongside specific design choices in lighting and sound to stage the moment when the central truth is finally revealed. Refer to specific staging choices and explain the effect on the audience at each stage.",
  "type": "20 marks — Extended Staging Response",
  "marks": "Mastering (17-20): The response demonstrates sustained, detailed application of physical theatre techniques alongside specific design choices. The build to the revelation is staged physically: the ensemble begins in a configuration of apparent domestic normality before performing a Frantic Assembly-influenced sequence that reveals the physical tensions beneath the surface (bodies beginning to resist, orbits contracting, a lift that fails or is withdrawn at the critical moment). The tableau at the revelation is precisely described — the physical positions of every performer, the spatial relationship between them, the use of levels (one performer on a raised platform, the others at floor level). Lighting uses a gobo pattern that fragments the stage during the ensemble sequence, then narrows to a single lighting special on the central figure at the tableau, with the surrounding performers in dim fill. Sound uses an overlapping soundscape of spoken fragments that collapses to silence at the revelation, or a sound cue that cuts abruptly. Costume, set or staging configuration may be addressed. Every choice is connected to physical theatre's communicative intention: the audience reads the family's fracture through bodies and space.\n\nSecure (13-16): Physical theatre techniques are applied with reasonable specificity across ensemble, lifts or chair duets, and tableau. At least two design disciplines are addressed. Effect on audience is explained.\n\nDeveloping (8-12): Physical theatre ideas are present but staging may be thin on specificity or miss design disciplines. Tableau may be noted but not precisely described.\n\nEmerging (1-7): Some knowledge of physical theatre is shown. Design choices are vague. Limited coherent staging strategy."
 }
]

